        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 12,
        "titulo": "Wollen: want to, and how it differs from möchten",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: «wollen» expresses a FIRM wish or a plan: «Ich will nächste Woche nach Berlin fahren» = I want to go to Berlin next week. False friend alert: «ich will» never means \"I will\". STEP 2: conjugate: ich will, du willst, er will, wir wollen, ihr wollt, sie wollen. Careful: «ich» and «er» are both «will», with no -t. STEP 3: the infinitive goes to the end. STEP 4: «möchten» is softer and more polite (\"I'd like\"); «wollen» is direct (\"I want\"). When asking a stranger for something, prefer «möchten»: «Ich möchte einen Kaffee», not «Ich will einen Kaffee», which sounds curt — just like \"I want a coffee\" does in English.",
